<MapContentLayout title="How to Create a Map With Multiple Pins: Step-by-Step" heading="How to Create a Map With Multiple Pins" description="Follow a six-step walkthrough to place pins, customize them, save your first map, and share or embed it on a website." intro="This walkthrough creates a small map for a museum visit. You’ll place a pin near the main entrance, add a second stop, and give each pin instructions a visitor can read. You can follow the same steps for a pair of branches, venues, or meeting points." kind="article">
  <article class="mm-reading prose-container readable-surface">
    <h2>What Is a Map With Multiple Pins?</h2>
    <p>It’s a single map that marks several places. A pin can point to a building entrance, a branch, or a place that doesn’t have a street address. Open any pin to see its own title and information.</p>
    <h2>How to Create a Map With Multiple Pins for Free</h2>
    <h3>Step 1. Open the Map Generator</h3><p>Open the <a href="/multiple-location-map-generator/">multiple location map generator</a>. Keep the live map visible as you work. The location list lets you inspect and reorder the pins.</p>
    <h3>Step 2. Add Your First Location</h3><p>Choose <strong>Enter Coordinates</strong> and enter <code>40.6712, -73.9636</code> for the Brooklyn Museum. If address search is available, you can instead look up the museum by name and select the matching address.</p>
    <h3>Step 3. Add More Locations</h3><p>To mark a precise meeting point, choose <strong>Add Pin on Map</strong>, click the spot, and enter a title. Select <strong>Fit All Locations</strong> to check the museum and meeting point together.</p>
    <figure><img src="/guides/native-multiple-map-editor.webp" width="1152" height="740" alt="The location list and MapLibre preview with three numbered pins on a map of New York" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>The example map matches three numbered pins to the location list.</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Step 4. Customize Your Pins</h3><p>Use <strong>Edit</strong> beside the meeting point and add a note such as “Meet outside the main doors at 10 a.m.” Choose a color or marker icon, then open the pin on the map to confirm what visitors will see.</p>
    <figure><img src="/guides/native-multiple-map-marker-editor.webp" width="544" height="616" alt="The Edit location dialog, showing title, description, coordinates, color, and marker icon" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Use the marker editor for the text and style visitors see.</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Step 5. Save Your Map</h3><p>Give the map a name such as <em>Museum visit meeting points</em>. Select <strong>Generate Map &amp; Embed Code</strong> and finish verification. Download the recovery file before leaving the editor.</p>
    <h3>Step 6. Share or Embed Your Map</h3><p>Use <strong>Copy Map URL</strong> to send a link to the group. If you manage an event page, use <strong>Copy Embed Code</strong> and paste it into a custom HTML block.</p>
    <h2>How to Import Multiple Locations From a CSV</h2><p>Put one location on each spreadsheet row and choose <strong>Import CSV</strong> in the editor. Preview the rows and check the matches. If CSV address lookup is unavailable, enter coordinates for any address-only rows. The <a href="/multiple-location-map-generator/#import-locations">CSV import instructions</a> explain the supported columns and how to correct a match.</p>
    <h2>How to Change Marker Colors</h2><p>Select <strong>Edit</strong> from the location list, choose a color, and save the location. Preview the marker on the selected basemap at the zoom visitors will see. See <a href="/multiple-location-map-generator/#customize-pins">examples of different marker styles</a> in the generator guide.</p>
    <h2>How to Embed a Map With Multiple Pins</h2><p>Leave responsive width enabled unless your page needs a fixed width. Copy the iframe to an embed or HTML block, and preview that page on both a phone and a desktop. If your publishing tool removes iframes, link to the shareable map instead.</p>
    <h2>Common Problems and Solutions</h2><ul><li><strong>The pin is in the wrong city.</strong> Search with the city and postcode, or use verified coordinates.</li><li><strong>Some pins are grouped in the editor.</strong> Open a cluster or zoom in to see the individual markers.</li><li><strong>The website still shows the old map.</strong> Reload after a minute. Public map data is briefly cached.</li></ul>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How do I add a pin for a place without an address?</summary><p>Use its latitude and longitude, or select the spot with Add Pin on Map.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I edit the map later on a different device?</summary><p>Yes. Move the recovery file securely to the other device and open it in the generator. Anyone holding the file can change or delete the map.</p></details>
  </article>
</MapContentLayout>
